From f7d9b30ee392ac8baf77922291cbfab42934290b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jasl <jasl9187@hotmail.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 16:12:16 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 15/23] fix: make the DSv4 mHC TileLang warmup actually run on
 CUDA, gated on config only
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The shipped warmup gated on hc_pre/hc_post attributes that only the AMD and XPU
layers expose, so on every CUDA build it returned without doing anything and the
mHC kernels JIT-compiled inside forward passes instead: a cold cache measured 13
serving-phase compiles per rank (~4s of nvcc each) under a 12-shape probe load,
all of them the two prenorm kernels recompiling per n_splits bucket. On a TP=2
GB10 node with ~5 GiB of headroom that nvcc spike is an OOM (wagzee's report).

The rewrite keeps the dummy-run design and changes what feeds it:

- Every decision is a pure function of vllm_config (model_type, hc_* hyperparams,
  platform, hidden_size/hc_mult from hf_config). _dummy_run issues TP collectives,
  so a gate that consults per-rank module state lets one rank skip a warmup
  another rank enters, deadlocking its first collective. That is not hypothetical:
  the v2 acceptance run deadlocked for 37 minutes because the test harness had
  deployed the fix to one node of two, and the stale rank's no-op gate skipped
  ahead — any per-rank skew, code or state, produces the same hang. Config-only
  gating makes the surviving skew surface as small as it can be.

- The ladder enumerates (n_splits, tile_n) compile keys, then drops keys already
  owned by cudagraph capture. num_tokens is a T.dynamic dimension and never keys
  a compilation, so capture size 32 owns the one cubin serving all of [17, 64] —
  filtering by size instead of key would redundantly warm that whole bucket.

- Dummy runs rather than direct kernel compilation, deliberately: variant
  selection is shape-dependent (the first local layer's 2-D residual picks the
  broadcast kernel), and the pre wrappers also JIT a DeepGEMM/Triton prenorm
  GEMM that factory.compile() would leave cold. A forward pass gets both right
  by construction; hand-built tensors already got both wrong once.

Acceptance on a cold TileLang cache, 2-node TP=2 GB10, DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731,
identical 12-shape probe load, cross-node tree parity asserted per arm:
  before: compiled_at_warmup=28  compiled_during_serving=18
  after:  compiled_at_warmup=52  compiled_during_serving=0
Warmup drove 11 dummy runs in 81.4 s (~11% of total boot) and compiled more key
coverage than the probe set exercises. Unit tests: 6 passed on GB10.

(cherry picked from commit 4eedf48765387f89791a2b5a3e457f2a8b8b1559)
